================================================================================
    /etc/environment CONFIGURATION FILE 
================================================================================

1. WHAT IS /etc/environment?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The `/etc/environment` file is a system-wide configuration file used in 
Linux operating systems to define global environment variables. 

Key Characteristics:
  - Global Scope : Variables apply to ALL users and background services.
  - PAM-Driven   : Read directly by Pluggable Authentication Modules (PAM) 
                   via `pam_env.so` during session initialization.
  - Non-Shell    : It is NOT a shell script; it is parsed as a simple configuration 
                   file before any user shell (Bash, Zsh, Fish) is loaded.

2. SYNTAX & FORMAT RULES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Because `/etc/environment` is parsed by PAM and not executed by a shell interpreter, 
it follows strict formatting rules:

  Rule 1: KEY=VALUE Format Only
          Syntax : VARIABLE_NAME="value"
          Example: JAVA_HOME="/usr/lib/jvm/java-11-openjdk"

  Rule 2: NO 'export' Keyword
          INCORRECT: export PATH="/usr/bin"
          CORRECT  : PATH="/usr/bin"

  Rule 3: NO Shell Expressions or Logic
          You CANNOT use conditional statements (if/then), functions, loops, or 
          command substitutions (like `$(date)` or `uname -r`).

  Rule 4: NO Variable Expansion/Referencing
          You CANNOT reference other variables inside this file.
          INCORRECT: PATH="$PATH:/opt/bin"
          CORRECT  : PATH="/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/opt/bin"


3. COMMON USE CASES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A. Setting System-Wide Search Path (PATH):
   PATH="/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin:/opt/bin"

B. Defining Default Software SDK/Runtime Locations:
   JAVA_HOME="/usr/lib/jvm/java-17-openjdk"
   GOROOT="/usr/local/go"

C. Configuring Global HTTP Proxy Settings:
   http_proxy="http://proxy.example.com:8080"
   https_proxy="http://proxy.example.com:8080"
   no_proxy="localhost,127.0.0.1,.example.com"

D. Defining Default System Locale & Language:
   LANG="en_US.UTF-8"
   LC_ALL="en_US.UTF-8"


4. COMPARISON: /etc/environment vs OTHER ENV CONFIG FILES
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+-------------------+----------------+----------------+--------------------------+
| File              | Scope          | Parsed By      | Supports Shell Logic?    |
+-------------------+----------------+----------------+--------------------------+
| /etc/environment  | System-wide    | PAM (pam_env)  | NO (Static KEY=VALUE)    |
| /etc/profile      | System-wide    | Login Shells   | YES (Full Bash syntax)   |
|/etc/profile.d/*.sh| System-wide    | Login Shells   | YES (Full Bash syntax)   |
| ~/.bashrc         | Per-user       | Interactive Bash| YES (Full Bash syntax)  |
| ~/.pam_environment| Per-user       | PAM (pam_env)  | NO (Deprecated in PAM)   |
+-------------------+----------------+----------------+--------------------------+


5. HOW TO APPLY & TEST CHANGES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 1: Edit the file (requires root privileges):
  sudo nano /etc/environment

Step 2: Save the file.

Step 3: Apply the changes:
  Because `/etc/environment` is read upon session creation, changes take effect 
  when a user logs in again.
  
  - For active terminal sessions: Log out and log back in, or re-authenticate:
      su - $USER
  
  - For system services: Restart the specific service (e.g., `sudo systemctl restart nginx`).

Step 4: Verify the variable:
  echo $JAVA_HOME
